+# Accounts service FMEA
+
+## Scope and scoring
+
+This assessment covers the current repository implementation and the operational context in `okf/`. It focuses on
+customer-visible account and payment behaviour, security, persistence, scheduled work, and the dependencies on the
+scheme gateway, fraud screening, core ledger and notifications.
+
+Scores are on a 1–10 scale. Severity (S) measures the impact if the failure occurs; occurrence (O) measures how likely
+it is without the recommended action; detection (D) measures how unlikely the failure is to be detected before harm
+(10 means poor detection). The risk priority number is `S × O × D`; higher numbers should be addressed first. Scores are
+qualitative release-triage estimates, not production incident rates.
+
+| # | Failure mode | Likely cause | Effect on customers and dependent systems/teams | Detection | S | O | D | RPN | Recommended action |
+|---:|---|---|---|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---|
+| 1 | Payment is sent without fraud screening | `PaymentService` calls `PaymentDispatcher` directly; the fraud client exists but is not wired into the payment flow, despite FRD-7. | Fraudulent or sanctioned payments can reach the scheme. Customers may lose funds; Fraud Operations lose the required review trail; Compliance and the bank face a serious control failure. | Compare every scheme submission with a fraud decision; alert on any missing decision; release-blocking integration test and audit report. | 10 | 9 | 8 | 720 | Insert screening before dispatch. Fail closed on timeout/error, map `REFER` to `HELD_FOR_REVIEW`, persist the decision and correlation id, and add an invariant that no payment reaches the scheme without an `ACCEPT`. |
+| 2 | A scheme timeout loses the local payment record although the scheme accepted it | The 10-second timeout can be after acceptance; `HttpSchemeClient` does not resolve the instruction and the transaction rolls back. | A payee may receive money while the customer sees an error and no usable payment record. Finance reconciliation sees a scheme instruction without a matching service/ledger record; support cannot investigate reliably. | Reconcile scheme settlement/instruction reports against payment ids daily; alert on unknown scheme references and elevated timeout rates. | 10 | 5 | 8 | 400 | On timeout, persist an indeterminate state and query `GET /v2/instructions/{reference}` before retrying. Make dispatch idempotent and retain the stable `P{id}` reference. |
+| 3 | Customer is debited twice during ledger recovery | A ledger timeout may mean the debit was booked. The code creates a new random `ledgerReference` during dispatch, so an operational retry can violate ledger idempotency. | Customer loses money twice; the ledger and account copy diverge; Finance must manually investigate a reconciliation break. | Match ledger postings by payment and reference; alert when one payment has more than one ledger reference or more than one debit. | 10 | 5 | 7 | 350 | Generate and persist `ledgerReference` before the first post; every retry must reuse it. Add a status-query/reconciliation workflow and forbid manual retry with a new reference. |
+| 4 | Payment is accepted by the scheme but never booked in the ledger | Scheme submission succeeds, then ledger posting fails or times out; the payment is marked `POSTING_FAILED` but there is no automated recovery. | Payment may settle while the ledger omits the debit. The local balance is reduced, the core balance is not; the customer view, Finance books and settlement report disagree. | Daily `POSTING_FAILED` exception report, scheme-to-ledger reconciliation at 07:00, and alert if an exception is not resolved within one business day. | 10 | 5 | 5 | 250 | Make `POSTING_FAILED` a durable work queue with ownership, retry using the same reference, and explicit reconciliation/compensation rules. Do not report completion until ledger outcome is known. |
+| 5 | A payment on a bank holiday is sent on an invalid date and rejected | `WorkingDays` excludes weekends only; bank holidays are not configured. The scheme rejects such instructions with `DT01`. | Customer expects the next working day but sees a rejection/delay. Queued payments and operations must be repaired; complaints and payment-timing commitments are affected. | Alert on `DT01`, compare queued execution dates with the bank-holiday calendar, and test all UK holidays around year-end. | 8 | 6 | 5 | 240 | Use a centrally managed UK bank-holiday calendar, calculate the next scheme working day, and monitor rejected queued payments by reason code. |
+| 6 | Concurrent payments overdraw or exceed the daily limit | Balance and daily-limit checks occur before save/dispatch, with no account lock or atomic reservation; requests can race. The balance is only a copy. | Multiple payments can be sent for the same funds or exceed policy. Ledger, scheme settlement and customer balance diverge; Finance and support need remediation. | Load/concurrency tests; compare daily scheme debits with limits and ledger balance; alert on negative/resync corrections. | 9 | 6 | 7 | 378 | Reserve funds atomically in the authoritative ledger (or use a transactional reservation/lock), enforce the daily limit there, and make duplicate client submissions idempotent. |
+| 7 | Queued payments remain unsent or are dispatched more than once | The 06:00 `@Scheduled` job has no durable claim/lease, retry strategy, or job outcome record; a failed instance or multi-instance deployment can miss or repeat work. | Customers miss promised execution dates, or a repeated submission can create operational confusion. Operations and customer support must chase overdue payments. | Alert on `QUEUED` payments past `executionDate`, record job runs and counts, and reconcile queued payments with scheme instructions. | 8 | 4 | 6 | 192 | Use a durable queue/outbox with leases, retry/backoff and idempotency; make scheduled work single-owner or safely claimable across instances. |
+| 8 | Customer-facing data is lost on restart or unavailable in production | Default datasource is in-memory H2 with `create-drop`; no production persistence, backup, restore or migration configuration is present. | Accounts, payments, cases, closure requests and statements can disappear or become unavailable. Customers lose history; Complaints, Finance and Regulatory Reporting lose evidence. | Startup configuration policy check, persistence smoke test across restart, backup-restore drill, and alert on unexpected database recreation. | 10 | 3 | 7 | 210 | Require an external durable database profile for production, schema migrations, backups and tested restore; fail startup when an in-memory datasource is used outside tests. |
+| 9 | Cross-tenant or over-privileged data is exposed | Tenant and role claims, key rotation, authority mapping, or resource checks can be misconfigured; access safety is concentrated in token claims and `AccessPolicy`. | A customer or staff member may view another bank's balances, transactions, cases or customer PII. This is a reportable security and data-protection incident. | Automated negative authorization matrix for every endpoint, tenant-boundary audit logs, and alerts on denied/access anomalies. | 10 | 3 | 8 | 240 | Add endpoint-by-endpoint tenant/role tests, issuer/audience/key-rotation validation, and security logging with immutable audit review. Keep 404 masking and tenant predicates at repository boundaries where possible. |
+| 10 | Full payee account details are returned to API clients | `PaymentView` includes `payeeSortCode` and `payeeAccountNumber`; the API response is not minimized even though notification policy requires last four digits only. | Sensitive payment data can be exposed in mobile logs, support tooling, caches or screenshots. Customers and the bank face privacy and breach-handling impact. | Contract test response schemas and DLP scanning of API/access logs; review client telemetry for account-number patterns. | 8 | 4 | 7 | 224 | Return only masked payee details or a safe payment token; prohibit full account numbers in logs, analytics and notifications, and add a data-classification contract test. |
+| 11 | Customer is not told that a payment was sent or failed | Notification delivery is best effort, failures are only logged, and there is no retry or delivery status. | Customer may think a payment did not happen, contact support, or submit a duplicate. Support volume rises; payment confidence and complaints handling suffer. | Monitor notification error rate and delivery acknowledgements; reconcile completed payments without a delivered notification. | 6 | 7 | 6 | 252 | Publish notifications through a durable outbox after the payment state is committed, retry safely, and expose delivery status to operations without coupling payment success to notification availability. |
+| 12 | Statements are missing or wrong for the regulatory return | `Statement` has storage classes but no generation/scheduling/API path in the service; monthly statements are required from ledger closing balances by the 5th working day. | Customers cannot obtain a reliable statement. Regulatory Reporting may submit an incomplete or incorrect return; Complaints cannot evidence account history. | Month-end completeness/reconciliation check for every open account and period; alert before the 5th working day; compare statement totals with ledger. | 9 | 6 | 8 | 432 | Implement an idempotent month-end statement job driven by ledger data, with completeness/total controls, rerun support and an operational deadline dashboard. |
+| 13 | Account closure is requested but cannot be safely completed | There is a request/list flow but no approval/decision endpoint or implementation shown, and the required zero-balance check against the ledger is not present. | Customers remain unable to close accounts, or a future implementation could close an account with funds stranded. Approvers, Operations and Complaints need manual intervention. | Monitor pending requests age; require ledger zero-balance evidence in approval audit records; alert on closure requests past SLA. | 9 | 5 | 7 | 315 | Implement two-person approval, tenant-scoped decision handling, ledger balance verification at decision time, idempotent account state transition, and an exception process for non-zero balances. |
+| 14 | Internal staff data or case history is returned to customers, or records are silently changed | Internal-note/comment filtering and write/audit controls are not represented in the exposed controllers; persistence entities have no immutable audit trail. | Risk scores, internal fraud notes or complaint evidence could be disclosed or altered. Customers, Fraud, Complaints and regulators lose trust in the record. | Authorization tests for every staff-only field, audit-log completeness checks, and periodic comparison of record history/change events. | 9 | 3 | 8 | 216 | Enforce separate customer/staff projections, explicit internal-field filtering, append-only audit events for notes/cases/payments, and retention/access review. |
+
+## Release priorities
+
+Before release, treat rows 1, 2, 8 and 12 as release-blocking because they combine either a direct financial/control
+failure or loss of required records. Rows 3, 4, 6, 11 and 13 require an owned operational runbook and alert before
+production traffic. The remaining controls should be tracked with an owner and due date, with the RPN recalculated from
+production incident and reconciliation data after launch.
+
